{:edb/db-before-t 949 :edb/db-after-t 950 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534262 50 #inst "2026-10-11T18:22:29.742Z" 13194139534262 17592186045417 true] [17592186162141 1038 #edb/ref 17592186416506 13194139534262 17592186045417 true] [17592186162442 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159854 13194139534262 17592186045417 false] [17592186162442 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416505 13194139534262 17592186045417 true] [17592186162442 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534262 17592186045417 true] [17592186162442 1071 "| $f(x, y)$ | $Y = 0$ | $Y = 1$ | $Y = 2$ | $f_{X}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X = 0$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X = 1$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.6$ |\n| $f_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ | $1$ |\n\nThe joint probability mass function $f(x, y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that\n$E[X] = 0.6, E[Y] = 1, SD[X] \\approx 0.4899, SD[Y] \\approx 0.8944$,\nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y$. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139534262 17592186045417 false] [17592186162442 1071 "| $f(x,y)$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.6$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ | $0.4$ | $1$ |\n\nThe joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=0.6,\\,\\text{E}[Y]=1,\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.4899,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.8944,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139534262 17592186045417 true] [17592186162442 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}xy\\,f(x,y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0\\cdot 0.1+0\\cdot 1\\cdot 0.1+0\\cdot 2\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & +1\\cdot 0\\cdot 0.3+1\\cdot 1\\cdot 0.1+1\\cdot 2\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =0.5-(0.6)(1) \\\\  & =-0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{-0.1}{0.4899\\cdot 0.8944} \\\\  & ≈0.23,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139534262 17592186045417 true] [17592186162446 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159878 13194139534262 17592186045417 false] [17592186162446 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416504 13194139534262 17592186045417 true] [17592186162446 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534262 17592186045417 true] [17592186162446 1071 "| $f(x, y)$ | $Y =-1$ | $Y = 1$ | $f_{X}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X = 0$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ | $0.7$ |\n| $X = 1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| $f_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nThe joint probability mass function $f(x, y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that\n$E[X] = 0.3E[Y] = 0.2, SD[X] \\approx 0.4583, SD[Y] \\approx 0.9798$,\nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y$. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139534262 17592186045417 false] [17592186162446 1071 "| $f(x,y)$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.2$ | $0.5$ | $0.7$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nThe joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=0.3\\,\\text{E}[Y]=0.2,\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.4583,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.9798,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139534262 17592186045417 true] [17592186162446 1074 "Recall that the correlation coefficient of random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance, and\n\n- $\\text{SD}[X]$ and $\\text{SD}[Y]$ are the standard deviations of $X$ and $Y.$\n\nFirst, by applying the rule of the lazy statistician, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}xy\\,f(x,y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 0.2+0\\cdot 1\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & +1\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 0.2+1\\cdot 1\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =-0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the covariance and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =-0.1-(0.3)(0.2) \\\\  & =-0.16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correlation coefficient is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}[X,Y]}{\\text{SD}[X]\\cdot \\text{SD}[Y]} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{-0.16}{0.4583\\cdot 0.9798} \\\\  & ≈-0.36,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139534262 17592186045417 true] [17592186416487 1043 #edb/ref 17592186416507 13194139534262 17592186045417 true] [17592186416487 1043 #edb/ref 17592186416508 13194139534262 17592186045417 true] [17592186416487 1043 #edb/ref 17592186416509 13194139534262 17592186045417 true] [17592186416487 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534262 17592186045417 true] [17592186416487 1045 "The Correlation Coefficient for Two Random Variables" 13194139534262 17592186045417 true] [17592186416487 1046 #edb/ref 17592186416508 13194139534262 17592186045417 true] [17592186416487 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046660 13194139534262 17592186045417 true] [17592186416487 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049206 13194139534262 17592186045417 true] [17592186416487 1053 #uu